{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "כי תשא", :translit "ki tisa", :gloss "when you take/lift"} {:he "רמה קרנם של ישראל בכי תשא", :translit "ramah karnam shel yisra'el bechi tisa", :gloss "their horn of Israel was raised in ki tisa"} {:he "כופר נפשו", :translit "kofer nafsho", :gloss "ransom of his soul"} {:he "יוה\"כ", :translit "yom hakippurim", :gloss "Yom Kippur"} {:he "תשא את ראש בני", :translit "tisa et rosh b'nei", :gloss "you shall take the head of the children of"} {:he "תר\"ג", :translit "tarag", :gloss "603"} {:he "ת\"ר אלף וג' אלפים", :translit "tav resh elef v'gimel alafim", :gloss "600 thousand and 3 thousand"} {:he "בנ\"י", :translit "b'nei yisra'el", :gloss "children of Israel"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition of ki tisa to prior section about raised horn of Israel"} {:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition of Yom Kippur to kofer nafsho; all the people pass before Him on Yom HaKippurim"} {:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "תשא את ראש בני", :spells "תר\"ג"} {:kind :gematria, :a "תשא את ראש בני", :b "תר\"ג"} {:kind :gematria, :a "בנ\"י", :b "תר\"ג"}], :frame {:text "The phrase 'ramah karnam' echoes the depiction of Israel's elevated status. The Mishnah states that on Yom HaKippurim all Israel pass before the Almighty like sheep, as stated in Psalms 33:15. The number 600,000 is the census figure for Israel given in Exodus 12:37 and throughout the wilderness narratives. The half-shekel collection described in Exodus 30:11-16 serves as a kofer nafsho, a ransom for each person's life.", :sources ["Mishnah Rosh HaShanah 1:2" "Psalms 33:15" "Exodus 12:37" "Exodus 30:12"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ונתנו", :translit "v'natnu", :gloss "and they shall give"} {:he "לך", :translit "lakh", :gloss "to you"} {:he "ג\"פ פקודים", :translit "gim'al p'kudim", :gloss "three times p'kudim (countings)"} {:he "ג\"פ מחצית השקל", :translit "gim'al machatzit hashekel", :gloss "three times half shekel"} {:he "ג\"פ כופר נפשו", :translit "gim'al kofer nafsho", :gloss "three times ransom of his soul"} {:he "ג\"פ לכפר", :translit "gim'al l'chapeir", :gloss "three times to atone"} {:he "ג\"פ בנ\"י", :translit "gim'al b'nei yisra'el", :gloss "three times children of Israel"} {:he "גד החוזה", :translit "gad hachozech", :gloss "Gad the seer"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Reading v'natnu in reverse yields the same letters — a palindrome: ונתנו"} {:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["פקודים"]} {:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["מחצית השקל"]} {:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["כופר נפשו"]} {:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["לכפר"]} {:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["בנ\"י לכפר"]} {:kind :other, :note "Gad the Seer offered David three punishments — war, famine, and pestilence — for the census he conducted"}], :frame {:text "The palindrome v'natnu teaches that what one gives returns — the Talmudic principle that charity causes no loss to the giver (Shabbat 151b). Gad the Seer presented David with three options of punishment after the census: three years of famine, three months of fleeing enemies, or three days of pestilence, as recorded in II Samuel 24:11-13 and I Chronicles 21:9-12. David's census brought plague upon Israel, linking counting to atonement for war, famine, and pestilence.", :sources ["Shabbat 151b" "II Samuel 24:11-13" "I Chronicles 21:9-12"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition of ki tisa to prior section about raised horn of Israel", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition of Yom Kippur to kofer nafsho; all the people pass before Him on Yom HaKippurim", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "תשא את ראש בני", :spells "תר\"ג", :letters ["ת" "א" "ר" "ב"], :by :selah, :order :mismatch} {:kind :gematria, :a "תשא את ראש בני", :a-value 1665, :b "תר\"ג", :b-value 603, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? false} {:kind :gematria, :a "בנ\"י", :a-value 62, :b "תר\"ג", :b-value 603, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? false} {:kind :o